# Engineering Rules

## Startup Tools
- On startup, procure the latest version of each required CRAP, mutation, and DRY tool for the project language directly from the listed `github.com/unclebob/...` repositories and get each one ready to run.
- Resolve each listed repository at its latest available upstream version before installing or building it.
- Do not rely on stale cached, vendored, or preinstalled copies when a fresh GitHub install/build is possible in the current environment.
- A project's language is what its own `swarmforge/language.conf` says - one word, the name
  this table uses - and nothing else decides it: not this layer, not the pack the project was
  composed from, not the default of any template. The file is the project's, written once and
  never overwritten, and a re-languaged project keeps its answer through every refresh and
  update. A project that has no such file has not said what it is written in yet: ask the
  operator rather than assuming, and write the answer into that file.
- A project's own helpers live in `swarmforge/local-scripts/`, which is on the path of
  every session and is a file the composition never writes: the shared scripts sit in
  `swarmforge/scripts/` and are replaced on every update, so a helper that belongs to
  this project and no other goes beside them rather than inside them.
- A project opens and closes through the forge that owns it: the dashboard's Open and
  Close, or `<forge>/swarmforge/scripts/swarmforge.sh --open-project <forge-root>
  <project>` beside it. Never start a project's own `./swarm` to open it, and never
  run a bare `./swarm` on a forge root: the first leaves the forge's list of open
  projects wrong - the dashboard, the phone and the Close guard all read it - and the
  second restarts the whole forge, stopping every project that list holds.
- Language tool table:
  - Go: install with `go install`; mutation `github.com/unclebob/mutate4go`, CRAP `github.com/unclebob/crap4go`, DRY `github.com/unclebob/dry4go`.
  - Clojure: install with Clojure CLI/deps.edn; mutation `github.com/unclebob/clj-mutate`, CRAP `github.com/unclebob/crap4clj`, DRY `github.com/unclebob/dry4clj`.
  - Java: build with Maven (`mvn`); mutation is PIT through the project's Maven profiles - the
    `mutate4java` repository is not used for Java/Maven projects, the way it is not used for Kotlin -
    CRAP is `github.com/sgo/crap4java`, the family's fork of it: `main` there mirrors upstream and the
    `forgelet` branch holds ours, which is what the tool registry's Java recipe installs, built as a
    jar. That branch is what our projects' scripts are written for — upstream never had
    `--test-command`, and its JaCoCo cannot read the Java 25 class files those projects compile to.
    DRY is `github.com/unclebob/dry4java`, which has no fork and needs none.
  - Kotlin: build with the project's own Gradle wrapper (`./gradlew`), never a Gradle from PATH.
    Mutation is PIT through its Gradle plugin — the `mutate4java` repository is not used, as it is not
    used for Java — CRAP is `github.com/JeevanThandi/slopguard-kotlin`, built from source by the tool
    registry's Kotlin recipe at the commit that recipe pins, DRY is detekt's duplication rule, and
    coverage is Kover (JetBrains'), which slopguard drives itself. The family's Java tools cannot serve
    Kotlin: `dry4java` parses Java source with JavaParser and `crap4java` drives Maven, so neither can
    see a `.kt` file. slopguard's score is its own weighted formula — `(cyc × cog) × (1 − cov/100)³ +
    sqrt(cyc × cog)`, default threshold 30 — not the classic `CC²(1−cov)³ + CC` that `crap4java`
    computes, so a Kotlin score and a Java score are not the same number.

## Language Defaults
- For Clojure projects, prefer Babashka where possible.
- For Clojure or Babashka projects, write Speclj specs, not `clojure.test`.
- Install Speclj with `swarm_tool.sh require speclj`. If missing, `swarm_tool.sh ensure speclj`.
- If a Speclj spec file changed, run `swarm_tool.sh require speclj-structure-check` (ensure if missing) and check the spec file before running tests.
- For Java projects, the Maven profiles are the contract: `swarm-mode` is the default and runs no gate,
  each gate has a profile of its own, and `human-mode` runs them together. Run a suite through the
  project's own script for that suite rather than a plain `mvn test`, and never treat a plain Maven
  invocation as a gate.
- For Kotlin projects, the wrapper is the build: run `./gradlew <task>`, never a Gradle from PATH, and
  never treat `./gradlew build` as a gate. Each suite has its own named task so a role runs only the
  suite it owns, and the gates stay off unless the role that owns one invokes it.

## Vertical Slices
- A card is one vertical slice: a public behaviour that can pass every required gate in the same
  card. It is not a layer of a behaviour.
- Split by behaviour, never by layer. "API", "storage", "execution", "unit tests", "property
  tests", "cleanup", "architecture", "mutation" and "verification" are not separate backlog items
  for one behaviour.
- Tests, cleanup, architecture, hardening and verification belong to the same slice. Do not defer
  a gate to a later card, and do not mark a slice complete because its code compiles.
- A slice has its own spec/task note/Gherkin, implementation, unit/property/architecture coverage,
  and every required gate. `stage complete` at the slice's tree is its completion.
- In a single-agent project, the first spec proposal for each card requires the operator's approval.
  After that first approval, corrections to the same unfinished card's task note or Gherkin are
  automatically approved by the stage tool. Completing the card resets the policy for the next card.
- A spec-only or bootstrap task is not a slice that skips gates. It needs an explicit waiver in its
  approved spec (and a `waived` record where the tool supports one), never a deferred gate.

## Java Gate Tasks
- A Java project provides its gates as Maven profiles, and the profiles are the contract every role uses:
  - `swarm-mode` - the default, with every gate off, so a plain `mvn` invocation runs no suite.
  - `-Pwith-unit-tests` - the unit suite.
  - `-Pwith-mutation-tests` - PIT, differential, with its history file kept.
   - `-Pwith-property-tests` - property tests, where the project has them, and never part of the unit
     suite.
   - `-Pwith-release-tests` - the gate a release activates: unit and property tests with no
     exclusion, and the profile the deploy step goes through.
   - the project's own acceptance profile - `with-acceptance-tests` or `acceptance`, whichever its
     scripts name - for the Gherkin acceptance suite.
  - `human-mode` - the gates together, for a person running the build by hand.
- A project's scripts name the profile for each suite; run a gate the way the script for it does, and
  leave the gates off otherwise. No gate is a homegrown substitute for the tool the table names.

## Documentation Gates
- A completed slice reviews three documents: `README.md` for installation, coordinates, quick start and
  project status; `docs/manual.md` for current public behaviour; and `ARCHITECTURE.md` for boundaries
  and structural decisions. The task records what changed in each or why it needed no change.
- `docs/manual-contract.edn` maps every maintained document section to its public APIs and executable
  examples. Each non-conceptual section runs at least one example against the current tree. A section
  may omit an example only by declaring itself conceptual with a reason.
- The required `docs` stage runs `scripts/check-documentation.sh`, which rejects missing sections, APIs,
  examples, required headings and broken local links. It runs after behavioural verification and before
  snapshot deployment, so documentation about the final tree is what ships.

## Java Snapshot Deployment
- A completed card for a project with `swarmforge/deploy.conf` publishes its current `-SNAPSHOT`
  after all verification gates and after the reviewed commit is pushed. Run
  `scripts/publish-snapshot.sh` through the required `deploy` stage; `stage complete` must refuse
  while that stage is absent, optional or stale.
- Snapshot deployment is not a release. It uses `-Pwith-release-tests deploy`, the project's
  token helper and the configured Maven repository, and it does not create a release tag or advance
  the development version. The optional `release` stage remains the separate `release:prepare` /
  `release:perform` path above.
- Every publishable template ships `scripts/publish-snapshot.sh`,
  `scripts/github-packages-token.sh` and `swarmforge/deploy.conf`. A project that genuinely has no
  deploy target removes `deploy.conf` and the deploy stage deliberately rather than leaving a
  configured target unimplemented.

## Java Releases
- A Java project's release is `mvn release:prepare` and then `mvn release:perform`, with the exact
  release version and the exact next development version the task names - never derived, never
  guessed. Never cut one with `versions:set`: it rewrites this project's own version and reads
  nothing else, so a release has shipped here depending on a SNAPSHOT of a published producer
  without a word from either command.
- Run `release_preflight.sh <release-version> <next-development-version>` first, and stop if it
  refuses. It checks what a release must not start without - a clean tree, a version still at
  `-SNAPSHOT` at HEAD, a release tag nobody has taken, no dependency on a SNAPSHOT this project
  does not build itself, a `<scm>` block (without one `mvn release:prepare` stops before it does
  anything), the `with-release-tests` profile the release activates, and, when
  `swarmforge/deploy.conf` names a target, a `<distributionManagement>` repository and the
  project's own token helper. When it passes it prints the two commands with the numbers the task
  named. The same two questions are the enforcer's `requireReleaseDeps` and
  `requireReleaseVersion` if a project wants its build to refuse on its own; the parent-snapshot
  half of that rule has to be configured off in the pom, since a module's SNAPSHOT parent is the
  normal state before a release.
- Pass `-DpushChanges=false` and push the branch and the tag as the release steps say, so the work
  travels the way every other card's does. That is why the perform half takes
  `-DlocalCheckout=true`: the tag prepare made exists only in this checkout while the push is still
  to come, and `release:perform` otherwise clones the SCM URL looking for a tag the remote has not got,
  failing with "Could not find remote branch v<version> to clone". Pushing the tag before perform would
  also work and would contradict pushing at the end, so the flag is what this recipe names.
- Activate the gate through `-Darguments`, not on the command line: `release:prepare` runs its own
  `clean verify` in a forked Maven that does not inherit a `-P`, so
  `... -Darguments="-Pwith-release-tests"` is what actually gates the release. Prepare runs before
  the tag and the version bump, so a red suite there stops the release with nothing tagged - which
  is why the gate belongs on that side. `release:perform` then checks the tag out and runs `deploy`,
  which would run the same suite a second time; cut that one with `-Darguments="-DskipTests"` and let
  the prepare half be the one that counts.
- Deploy only when `swarmforge/deploy.conf` names a target, through the project's own token
  helper, the way the deploy step does everywhere. A deploy that fails means no tag and no next
  development version: report it instead of continuing.

## Kotlin Gate Tasks
- A Kotlin project's build provides these tasks through its own wrapper, and these names are the
  contract every role uses:
  - `unitTest` — the unit suite.
  - `acceptanceTest` — the Gherkin acceptance suite, through the acceptance runner.
  - `propertyTest` — property tests, and never part of `unitTest`.
  - `mutation` — PIT, differential, with its history file kept.
  - `coverage` — Kover, producing the report CRAP reads.
  - `crap` — slopguard-kotlin over the changed sources, reading Kover's report rather than running
    coverage again.
- The build keeps them separate and off unless invoked: a role runs only the gate it owns, and no gate
  is a homegrown substitute for the tool the table names.

## Design And Testability
- Work in small, reviewable increments.
- Prefer the simplest design that supports the current behavior and leaves clear options for the next step.
- Keep tests close to the behavior being changed.
- Separate testable modules from environmentally unsuitable modules that open GUIs, depend on external devices, throw environment errors, emit system errors, or hang under automated tests. Maximize testable code and minimize the unsuitable boundary.
- IO-near modules must not reimplement a domain question. If a high-level module already answers it, call that module and translate the result. Walking the same facts again is a defect even when the dependency arrow already points inward.
- Only testable modules should participate in tools that run tests, including unit tests, acceptance tests, coverage, mutation testing, CRAP analysis, DRY analysis that invokes tests, and property tests.
- Keep property tests separate from normal verification. Do not include property-test tags in normal unit coverage, Gherkin acceptance mutation, language mutation tools, CRAP, or coverage commands unless the role owns property-test verification or the user explicitly asks for property tests.

## Acceptance Pipeline
- Use github.com/unclebob/Acceptance-Pipeline-Specification for Gherkin acceptance tests.
- The family's APS fork is `github.com/sgo/Acceptance-Pipeline-Specification`, branch `forgelet`.
  Prefer its `gherkin-parser`, `ir-dry-checker` and `gherkin-mutator` over upstream; upstream is
  only the fallback when the fork cannot be used.
- The Acceptance Pipeline Specification supplies `gherkin-parser`, `ir-dry-checker`, and `gherkin-mutator`. Install them with the project-local helper; do not search `$HOME` or run `find` for binaries.
- At Tool Startup, for each required APS tool, run `swarm_tool.sh require <tool>`. If it is missing, run exactly `swarm_tool.sh ensure <tool>`.
- Two-arg forms:
  - `gherkin-parser <feature> ./tmp/<stem>.json`
  - `ir-dry-checker <ir> ./tmp/<stem>.dry.json`
- Prefer the Babashka APS tools for `gherkin-parser`, `ir-dry-checker`, `gherkin-mutator`, and related APS support commands.
- Use Go-based APS tools only if the Babashka APS tools do not work in the current project environment.
- Project-specific acceptance pipeline components are the acceptance entrypoint generator, acceptance runtime, project step handlers, runner adapter, and convenience scripts.
- An approved feature is executed, not merely translated into hand-written tests. The project's
  acceptance runner must parse every approved feature, dispatch every step to a handler that calls
  the public behaviour, and fail on an unhandled or ambiguous step. Hand-written unit or property
  tests are not acceptance evidence.
- Gherkin acceptance mutation means running `gherkin-mutator` to mutate Gherkin example values.
- Write value-driven behaviour as Gherkin Scenario Outlines with example tables. `gherkin-mutator`
  mutates example cells only, so a feature with no examples produces zero mutations and is not
  acceptance-mutation evidence. A boolean scenario may stay a plain scenario; record that choice in
  the task note. When a non-waived feature reports zero mutations, parameterize its value-driven
  scenarios or record the exemption.
- Gherkin acceptance mutation runs must report periodic progress/status so agents can distinguish normal long-running work from a hang.

## Verification
- Before running language, build, or test commands, prefer project-local cache/configuration paths inside the assigned worktree. Avoid default cache locations that write outside the project and may trigger sandbox or permission restrictions.
- Run constitution tools one at a time. Do not run CRAP, DRY, coverage, language mutation, Gherkin mutation, or structure-check concurrently.
- Tools that take a worker limit must use `--max-workers 4` or `--workers 4`.
- Function mutation is differential against the source manifest. Do not pass `--mutate-all`.
- Scan changed and new source files with the language mutation tool's scan/count mode as a hint that a module may mix jobs. Split a source file when it has more than one job. Do not split a one-job module to chase a mutation-site count. Preserve mutation manifests across the split; do not hand-edit them.
- Gherkin mutation is differential against the feature manifest (`--level hard`). Do not pass `--level full`.
- Run acceptance generation and acceptance tests sequentially.
- Avoid running whole-suite language test commands concurrently with acceptance generation.
- Run the relevant local verification command before handoff whenever the project has one.

## Guardrails
- Do not invent project-local CRAP, DRY, mutation, or coverage proxies. Install and run the constitution tools (`crap4clj` with cloverage, `dry4clj`, `clj-mutate`, speclj, or the language table). Do not treat a homegrown `bb crap` / `bb coverage` / `bb mutation-count` task as those tools.
- Do not edit mutation testing or Gherkin acceptance mutation manifests by hand; allow approved mutation tools to update those manifests as part of their normal runs.
- Do not commit unrelated local changes or generated artifacts unless required for the task.
- Before relying on an unfamiliar command, inspect local help or project documentation.
